1. Problem Statement — LeetCode 491

You are given an integer array nums.

You need to find all unique non-decreasing subsequences of length at least 2.

Non-decreasing means:

The next element should be:

greater than or equal to the previous element

Example:

nums = [4, 6, 7, 7]

Some valid subsequences:

[4, 6]
[4, 7]
[4, 6, 7]
[4, 6, 7, 7]
[6, 7]
[7, 7]

Invalid:

[6, 4] ❌

because:

6 > 4

Also, duplicate subsequences should not appear.

2. Intuition

For every position, we have a choice:

Should I include this element in my current subsequence?

Suppose:

nums = [4, 6, 7]

Start with:

temp = []

Choose 4:

[4]

Then choose 6:

[4, 6]

Since its size is at least 2, it is a valid answer:

[4, 6] ✅

Then continue:

[4, 6, 7] ✅

But we can also skip 6 and choose 7:

[4, 7] ✅

So we need to explore all possible combinations, while maintaining the original order.

This naturally suggests:

Backtracking / Recursion


Logic:

The problem asks us to find all unique non-decreasing subsequences with a length of at least two.
Since we need to explore different combinations while maintaining the original order, I used a backtracking approach.
At each recursion level, I try every element starting from the current index.
Before adding an element, I check whether it maintains the non-decreasing order by comparing it with the last element of the current subsequence.
Whenever the current subsequence has at least two elements, I add a copy of it to the result. After exploring one choice, I remove that element to backtrack and try other possibilities.
To avoid duplicate subsequences, I use a HashSet at each recursion level so that the same value is not selected more than once at the same level

Code:

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> findSubsequences(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans=new ArrayList<>();
        subseq(nums,0,new ArrayList<>(),ans);
        return ans;
    }
    void subseq(int[] nums,int index,List<Integer> temp,List<List<Integer>> ans){
        if(temp.size()>=2){
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(temp));
        }
        HashSet<Integer> set=new HashSet<>();
        for(int i=index;i<nums.length;i++){
            if(set.contains(nums[i])){
                continue;
            }
            if(temp.isEmpty() || temp.get(temp.size()-1)<=nums[i]){
                set.add(nums[i]);
                temp.add(nums[i]);
                subseq(nums,i+1,temp,ans);
                temp.remove(temp.size()-1);
            }
        }
    }
}
